00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.370 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  throughout the first half of 2018 the  European Parliament led a series of  debates on the future of Europe with EU  leaders from migration and climate  change to growth and security eight  leaders have come to Parliament to share  their views on the challenges facing the  bloc one of these challenges is the UK's  decision to leave the EU Parliament  plays a key role in negotiating the  withdrawal agreement in March members  called for joint commitments on citizens  rights financial obligations and the  Irish border in continued efforts  towards a stronger digital economy  Parliament voted to end barriers to  cross-border online shopping and  approved a draft law to facilitate the  free flow of non personal data  strengthening the rights of posted  workers members voted on new rules to  ensure workers who were sent temporarily  to other EU countries get the same pay  for the same work  the aim is to improve their travel and  accommodation conditions and establish  fair competition for companies at least  35% that's how much energy should stem  from renewable sources members also set  objectives like the use of smart  technologies and the energy-efficient  renovation of buildings as part of the  fight against climate change the e used  carbon trading system was updated to cut  greenhouse gas emissions by at least 40  percent and further curb co2 emissions  from industry while encouraging  low-carbon investment Parliament set new  targets to boost recycling cut landfill  and prevent waste through better  packaging in its efforts to move towards  a resource efficient society the new  rules also aim to reduce food waste in  the EU by Harvey members call for a  sustainable farm policy to be integrated  into the circular economy fostering  innovation research and smart practices  they urged fairness in distributing  funds to farmers and called for  increased support for beekeepers  [Music]

